{"type": "step", "node_id": 27, "parent_node_id": 26, "role": "assistant", "content": "We need all integers $t$ with $(3t+10)\\mid(t^3-t^2)$.\n\n**Polynomial reduction.** Divide $t^3-t^2$ by $3t+10$. Working with $27(t^3-t^2)$ to clear fractions:\n\n$$27(t^3-t^2)=(3t+10)(9t^2-39t+130)-1300.$$\n\nSo $(3t+10)\\mid 27(t^3-t^2)+1300$, i.e. $(3t+10)\\mid 27(t^3-t^2)$ iff $(3t+10)\\mid 1300$.\n\nSince $3t+10\\equiv 1\\pmod 3$, we have $3\\nmid(3t+10)$, hence $\\gcd(27,3t+10)=1$. Therefore\n\n$$(3t+10)\\mid(t^3-t^2)\\iff (3t+10)\\mid 1300.$$\n\n**Finding divisors.** $1300=2^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 13$. The condition $3t+10\\equiv 1\\pmod 3$ is automatic (since $3t+10=3(t+3)+1$), so we need divisors $d$ of $1300$ with $d\\equiv 1\\pmod 3$.\n\nPositive divisors: $1,2,4,5,10,13,20,25,26,50,52,65,100,130,260,325,650,1300$.\n\nReducing mod $3$: $1,2,1,2,1,1,2,1,2,2,1,2,1,1,2,1,2,1$.\n\nThose $\\equiv 1\\pmod 3$: $\\{1,4,10,13,25,52,100,130,325,1300\\}$.\n\nFor negative divisors $-d$: since each positive $d\\equiv 1$, we get $-d\\equiv 2\\pmod 3$, so none qualify.\n\n**Computing $t=(d-10)/3$:**\n\n| $d$ | 1 | 4 | 10 | 13 | 25 | 52 | 100 | 130 | 325 | 1300 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| $t$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $5$ | $14$ | $30$ | $40$ | $105$ | $430$ |\n\n**Verification (spot checks).** $t=-3$: $(-27-9)/1=-36$ ✓. $t=-2$: $(-8-4)/4=-3$ ✓. $t=0$: $0/10=0$ ✓. $t=430$: $430^2\\cdot429/1300=1849\\cdot33=61017$ ✓.\n\n**Sum:**\n$$-3-2+0+1+5+14+30+40+105+430=620.$$\n\nThe set is $S=\\{-3,-2,0,1,5,14,30,40,105,430\\}$, and the sum of all elements is $\\boxed{620}$.\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "The problem: find all integers t such that (t^3 - t^2)/(3t+10) is an integer. Let S be the set, sum all elements.\n\nLet me solve. Let d = 3t+10. Then t = (d-10)/3, requires d ≡ 10 mod 3, i.e., d ≡ 1 mod 3.\n\nWe need (3t+10) | (t^3 - t^2).\n\nPolynomial division: t^3 - t^2 divided by 3t+10.\n\nLet me do the division. t^3 - t^2 = (3t+10)·Q(t) + R where R is constant.\n\nQ(t): leading t^3/(3t) = (1/3)t^2. (3t+10)·(1/3)t^2 = t^3 + (10/3)t^2. Subtract: -t^2 - (10/3)t^2 = -(13/3)t^2.\n\nNext: -(13/3)t^2/(3t) = -(13/9)t. (3t+10)·(-(13/9)t) = -(13/3)t^2 - (130/9)t. Subtract: (130/9)t.\n\nNext: (130/9)t/(3t) = 130/27. (3t+10)·(130/27) = (130/9)t + 1300/27. Subtract: -1300/27.\n\nSo t^3 - t^2 = (3t+10)·[(1/3)t^2 - (13/9)t + 130/27] - 1300/27.\n\nMultiply by 27: 27(t^3-t^2) = (3t+10)·[9t^2 - 39t + 130] - 1300.\n\nSo (3t+10) | 27(t^3 - t^2) iff (3t+10) | 1300.\n\nBut we need (3t+10) | (t^3 - t^2), not just 27 times. Since gcd(27, 3t+10): 3t+10 ≡ 1 mod 3, so 3 doesn't divide 3t+10, thus gcd(27, 3t+10) = 1 (well, 3t+10 could share factor with 27 only if 3 divides it, which it doesn't). So gcd(27, 3t+10)=1.\n\nTherefore (3t+10) | (t^3 - t^2) iff (3t+10) | 1300.\n\nWait let me double check. We have 27(t^3-t^2) = (3t+10)·M - 1300 where M = 9t^2-39t+130 integer. So 27(t^3-t^2) + 1300 = (3t+10)·M. Thus (3t+10) | 27(t^3-t^2) + 1300. If (3t+10)|(t^3-t^2), then (3t+10)|27(t^3-t^2), so (3t+10)|1300. Conversely if (3t+10)|1300, then (3t+10)|27(t^3-t^2) (since 27(t^3-t^2) = (3t+10)M - 1300, both terms divisible), and gcd(27,3t+10)=1 so (3t+10)|(t^3-t^2). Good.\n\nSo need 3t+10 divides 1300, and 3t+10 ≡ 1 mod 3 (automatically since 3t+10 = 3t+9+1 ≡1 mod 3).\n\nDivisors of 1300 = 2^2 · 5^2 · 13. Number of divisors = 3·3·2 = 18. Divisors d with d ≡ 1 mod 3.\n\nList all divisors of 1300: 1, 2, 4, 5, 10, 13, 20, 25, 26, 50, 52, 65, 100, 130, 260, 325, 650, 1300.\n\nCheck mod 3:\n1→1 ✓\n2→2\n4→1 ✓\n5→2\n10→1 ✓\n13→1 ✓\n20→2\n25→1 ✓\n26→2\n50→2\n52→1 ✓\n65→2\n100→1 ✓\n130→1 ✓\n260→2\n325→1 ✓\n650→2\n1300→1 ✓\n\nSo d ∈ {1, 4, 10, 13, 25, 52, 100, 130, 325, 1300}.\n\nAlso negative divisors: -1, -4, -10, -13, -25, -52, -100, -130, -325, -1300. Need d ≡ 1 mod 3. -1 ≡ 2, -4 ≡ 2, -10 ≡ 2, -13 ≡ 2, -25 ≡ 2, -52 ≡ 2, -100 ≡ 2, -130 ≡ 2, -325 ≡ 2, -1300 ≡ 2. All negatives are ≡ 2 mod 3 (since positives are ≡1, negation gives ≡2). So none of negatives work.\n\nSo d values: 1, 4, 10, 13, 25, 52, 100, 130, 325, 1300.\n\nt = (d-10)/3:\nd=1: -9/3 = -3\nd=4: -6/3 = -2\nd=10: 0/3 = 0\nd=13: 3/3 = 1\nd=25: 15/3 = 5\nd=52: 42/3 = 14\nd=100: 90/3 = 30\nd=130: 120/3 = 40\nd=325: 315/3 = 105\nd=1300: 1290/3 = 430\n\nS = {-3, -2, 0, 1, 5, 14, 30, 40, 105, 430}.\n\nSum = -3-2+0+1+5+14+30+40+105+430.\n-3-2 = -5; -5+1 = -4; -4+5 = 1; 1+14 = 15; 15+30 = 45; 45+40 = 85; 85+105 = 190; 190+430 = 620.\n\nSum = 620.\n\nLet me verify a couple: t=-3: t^3-t^2 = -27-9 = -36. 3t+10 = 1. -36/1 = -36 ✓.\nt=-2: -8-4=-12. 3t+10=4. -12/4=-3 ✓.\nt=430: 3t+10 = 1300. t^3-t^2 = 430^2·429 = 184900·429. 184900·429/1300 = 184900/1300 · 429 = 142.23... wait 1300·142 = 184600, remainder 300. Hmm let me recompute. 184900/1300: 1300·142 = 184600, 184900-184600=300. So not integer? Let me recheck.\n\nWait, t^3 - t^2 = t^2(t-1) = 430^2 · 429 = 184900 · 429. 184900·429 = let me compute: 184900·400 = 73,960,000; 184900·29 = 5,362,100; total = 79,322,100. Divide by 1300: 79,322,100/1300 = 7932210/130 = 61,017? 130·61017 = 7,932,210. Yes! = 61017. ✓ Good, I made arithmetic error before; 184900/1300 isn't integer but the product with 429 makes it work because 1300 = 4·325... anyway 429 = 3·143, and 1300 = 4·325. 184900 = 4·46225 = 4·5^2·1849 = 4·25·1849. 1849 = 43^2. So 184900 = 2^2·5^2·43^2. 1300 = 2^2·5^2·13. 184900/1300 = 43^2/13 = 1849/13 = 142.23... not integer. But times 429 = 3·143 = 3·11·13. So 184900·429/1300 = (43^2·429)/13 = 1849·429/13 = 1849·33 = 61017. ✓.\n\nGreat, sum = 620.", "tool_calls": [], "created_at": 1787222573}
